[stderr] +This workspace is a **planning and runbook-authoring factory** for an eventual
[stderr] +implementation project. It does not contain a game implementation, product
[stderr] +test evidence, deployment, publishing, or release approval. The factory turns
[stderr] +the approved brief into bounded, reviewable implementation contracts; a later
[stderr] +implementation project chooses its stack and carries out those contracts only
[stderr] +after their gates are approved.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Read the sources in this order
[stderr] +
[stderr] +1. [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md) is the original source of truth. It records the
[stderr] +   scope, selected targets, constraints, non-goals, and unanswered questions.
[stderr] +   [`PROJECT_BRIEF.md`](PROJECT_BRIEF.md) is its durable project-local copy.
[stderr] +2. The canonical [specification index](specification/README.md) is the stable
[stderr] +   requirement and decision system. Start with the [domain-discovery map]
[stderr] +   (specification/00-domain-discovery.md): it explains why exploration, town,
[stderr] +   dungeon, combat, inventory, quests, save/recovery, accessibility, web, and
[stderr] +   PWA systems appear in the downstream runbooks. The canonical specification
[stderr] +   must not override the original brief.
[stderr] +3. The planning catalogue—[`implementation-catalogue-index.md`](planning/implementation-catalogue-index.md)
[stderr] +   and its authoritative [JSON](planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json)—
[stderr] +   maps canonical requirements to bounded `IMP-*` contracts. The audited
[stderr] +   [delivery map](planning/05-delivery-map.md) and
[stderr] +   [contract audit](planning/09-contract-audit.md) provide dependency and
[stderr] +   closure evidence.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Responsive public web and installable PWA delivery are included by default for
[stderr] +this project. All actual platform requirements, including input, storage,
[stderr] +offline/update, browser support, and performance constraints, come from the
[stderr] +selected targets in the original brief and the canonical platform appendix;
[stderr] +they are not inferred from "web/PWA" alone.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## The three levels of work
[stderr] +
[stderr] +| Level | Purpose | Inputs and outputs | Not permission to |
[stderr] +| --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] +| Source brief and canonical specification | Preserve P0 scope, requirements, decisions, risks, target adaptations, and traceability | `INITIAL.md`, `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, `specification/`, and `planning/` | Select gated technology, resolve an open decision, or build/release the game |
[stderr] +| B-series authoring runbooks | Write bounded `R####` implementation contracts from allocated `IMP-*` records | `authoring-runbooks/`, `planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json`, and its Markdown ledger | Implement product code, create final assets, or claim product evidence |
[stderr] +| R-series implementation runbooks | Give the eventual implementation project one small, independently verifiable contract at a time | Repository-root `runbooks/R####.md` and the evidence paths named by each contract | Bypass its dependencies or human/external gates, publish, deploy, or release |
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The `Final Fantasy V` reference is a high-level party-based, turn-based
[stderr] +adventure scope reference only. Never copy or request its branding, names,
[stderr] +story/lore, dialogue, maps, art, music, layouts, combat screens, or distinctive
[stderr] +interaction choreography. All creative work must be independently original and
[stderr] +follow the warm hand-painted, readable-silhouette, restrained-palette direction
[stderr] +with provenance and human creative review.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Run the factory and authoring collections
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Run these from the repository root. `--project mock-fantasy-quest-4` loads this
[stderr] +workspace's versioned, non-secret Supervisor configuration and uses its durable
[stderr] +state. An accepted task is skipped on a later collection run unless explicitly
[stderr] +retried.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 1. Factory collection
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Run the F-series factory collection and discover its registered child
[stderr] +collections:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +./supervisor/.venv/bin/supervisor-run --project mock-fantasy-quest-4 --run-all --runbooks-dir runbooks
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The factory source collection is repository-root `runbooks/`; its child
[stderr] +registration is `runbooks/.supervisor-children/mock-fantasy-quest-4.json`.
[stderr] +The project factory state and raw, per-stage logs are under
[stderr] +`.state/factory.sqlite3` and `.state/live/` respectively.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 2. B-series implementation-contract authoring
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Run the current B-series collection after its prerequisites are accepted:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +./supervisor/.venv/bin/supervisor-run --project mock-fantasy-quest-4 --run-all --runbooks-dir projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +To run just a dependency-ready writer, use its immutable file—for example:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +./supervisor/.venv/bin/supervisor-run --project mock-fantasy-quest-4 --runbook projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0001.md
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The authoring collection starts at `authoring-runbooks/INITIAL.md`; the
[stderr] +dispatcher is `authoring-runbooks/D0001.md`. The B-series collection has its
[stderr] +own `.state/authoring-runbooks.sqlite3` and `.state/live/` evidence locations.
[stderr] +Its authoritative allocation ledger is
[stderr] +[`planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json),
[stderr] +with [`planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md`](planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md)
[stderr] +as the readable companion. Each B writer may create only its manifest-owned
[stderr] +R-series paths.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Validate the generated collection after each bounded wave:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +python3 projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py --wave B0001
[stderr] +python3 projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The validator is structural authoring evidence only. Its report is terminal
[stderr] +output; retain the relevant Supervisor task result, live log, and manifest
[stderr] +checkpoint in `.state/` and `planning/`. It does not prove implementation,
[stderr] +browser behaviour, accessibility, visual quality, or release readiness.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 3. R-series implementation contracts
[stderr] +
[stderr] +After the responsible B writer has generated an R contract, dispatch it to the
[stderr] +eventual implementation project through the chosen **Codex-only Supervisor
[stderr] +pipeline** (`SUPERVISOR_AGENT_ORDER=codex`, followed by Supervisor's configured
[stderr] +independent precheck/test/browser/visual-review/completion gates). For one
[stderr] +selected contract:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +./supervisor/.venv/bin/supervisor-run --project mock-fantasy-quest-4 --runbook runbooks/R0001.md
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +For all currently generated R-series contracts, run each manifest-owned file
[stderr] +in its declared dependency order (only after every file exists):
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```zsh
[stderr] +for runbook in runbooks/R*.md; do
[stderr] +  ./supervisor/.venv/bin/supervisor-run --project mock-fantasy-quest-4 --runbook "$runbook"
[stderr] +done
[stderr] +```
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The R contracts are generated in repository-root `runbooks/`, while their
[stderr] +implementation evidence belongs only at the test, browser, visual, and other
[stderr] +paths named by each individual R contract. Do not treat a planned path or a
[stderr] +passing authoring validator as implementation evidence. The implementation
[stderr] +project must retain its own run state, test reports, browser artefacts, visual
[stderr] +review, and any human approval records at those declared locations.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Scaling and safe resume
[stderr] +
[stderr] +For a larger project, repeat this controlled wave:
[stderr] +
[stderr] +1. Run one dispatcher wave (`D####`). It may expand at most one eligible
[stderr] +   chapter, then allocate a bounded group of B-series writers.
[stderr] +2. Run only the newly allocated, dependency-ready B-series tasks. Keep every
[stderr] +   B batch at seven or fewer R contracts and each dispatcher wave at ten or
[stderr] +   fewer B writers.
[stderr] +3. Run the structural validator through the last affected B batch, checkpoint
[stderr] +   accepted work, and repeat from the manifest's first unaccepted batch.
[stderr] +4. When no queued or ready work remains, run the one final-audit B task the
[stderr] +   dispatcher creates. It audits the authoring system; it is not an
[stderr] +   implementation or release audit.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Never renumber, regenerate, mutate, or overwrite an accepted B/R contract.
[stderr] +For an approved scope change, add a new dispatcher wave and new monotonic
[stderr] +catalogue/B/R IDs rather than altering accepted contracts. Stop the affected
[stderr] +work for human review when a source conflict, missing coverage, cycle,
[stderr] +ID/output collision, credentials, paid service, legal/compliance decision,
[stderr] +device/store access, deployment, publishing, or release approval is involved.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Safe resume points are the `checkpoint` and `state` fields in the authoritative
[stderr] +manifest, the first non-accepted B batch, durable state databases in `.state/`,
[stderr] +and the latest corresponding `.state/live/` log. Preserve unresolved questions
[stderr] +in `specification/decision-log.md` and retain their `GATE-*` records; recording
[stderr] +a gate is never approval to proceed.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Directory and evidence map
[stderr] +
[stderr] +| Location | Role |
[stderr] +| --- | --- |
[stderr] +| `INITIAL.md`, `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` | Original and durable source briefs; the original brief wins on conflict |
[stderr] +| `specification/` | Canonical requirements, decisions, traceability, platform appendix, domain-completeness review, and specification index |
[stderr] +| `planning/` | Delivery map, implementation catalogue, allocation manifest/ledger, authoring handoff, audit, and structural validator |
[stderr] +| `planning/runbook-generation-quality-gate.md` and validator output | The structural validation procedure and its per-wave terminal report; retain the corresponding Supervisor result and live log as durable evidence |
[stderr] +| `authoring-runbooks/` | `INITIAL.md`, immutable B-series writers, dispatchers, and child-collection registrations |
[stderr] +| `runbooks/` | F-series factory runbooks plus generated R-series implementation contracts; its `.supervisor-children/` registers this project authoring collection |
[stderr] +| `.state/factory.sqlite3`, `.state/authoring-runbooks.sqlite3` | Durable Supervisor task state for the factory and authoring collections |
[stderr] +| `.state/live/` | Raw per-stage Supervisor evidence logs; use reports/state commands configured by Supervisor to inspect durable evidence |
[stderr] +| Paths declared by an R contract | Future implementation tests, browser artefacts, visual-review evidence, recovery evidence, and human-gate records; no such artefacts are created by authoring alone |
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The current safe boundary is an original, offline-capable, single-player 2D
[stderr] +fantasy RPG with one town, one dungeon, a short guided campaign and ending.
[stderr] +Connected identity/account state, multiplayer and safety services, live
[stderr] +operations, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics/monetisation, seasonal
[stderr] +events, and player-created/shared content remain excluded or held until a new
[stderr] +approved product, privacy, security, operational, cost, and failure-mode
[stderr] +contract exists.
[stderr] 
